q('P','async await','Return await inside try','What does this function’s caller log?','recovered','await observes the rejection inside the try block.~The catch returns recovered.~The async function therefore fulfills with that string. Returning the rejecting promise without awaiting would bypass this local catch.','Locate where rejection becomes a throw.','Is the await inside the protected region?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Operators/await#handling_rejected_promises','async function load() {\n  try {\n    return await Promise.reject(new Error("offline"));\n  } catch {\n    return "recovered";\n  }\n}\nload().then(value => console.log(value));')
q('P','Promises','First settlement wins','What does this promise log?','first','The executor calls resolve with first.~Later resolve and reject calls cannot change the settled outcome.~The registered fulfillment handler receives first. Executor code after resolve still executes, even though it cannot resettle the promise.','Settlement is one-way.','Does resolve stop the executor body?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Promise/Promise#the_resolve_function','new Promise((resolve, reject) => {\n  resolve("first");\n  resolve("second");\n  reject(new Error("late"));\n}).then(value => console.log(value));')
q('T','Async iteration','Async iterator cleanup','What does breaking this async loop log?','1\ncleanup\nfinished','The consumer receives and logs 1.~break requests iterator closure and awaits its return behavior.~The generator’s finally completes before the loop’s following statement.','Early exit closes the iterator.','Does for await wait for cleanup?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Statements/for-await...of#description','async function* source() {\n  try {\n    yield 1;\n    yield 2;\n  } finally {\n    await Promise.resolve();\n    console.log("cleanup");\n  }\n}\n(async () => {\n  for await (const value of source()) {\n    console.log(value);\n    break;\n  }\n  console.log("finished");\n})();')
q('W','Concurrency','Bounded async mapping','Implement mapLimit(items, limit, fn) with at most limit pending fn calls, ordered results, and no new calls after an observed failure. Already-started calls may finish.','async function mapLimit(items, limit, fn) {\n  if (!Number.isInteger(limit) || limit < 1) {\n    throw new RangeError("limit must be a positive integer");\n  }\n  const results = new Array(items.length);\n  let cursor = 0;\n  let stopped = false;\n  async function worker() {\n    while (!stopped && cursor < items.length) {\n      const index = cursor++;\n      try {\n        results[index] = await fn(items[index], index);\n      } catch (error) {\n        stopped = true;\n        throw error;\n      }\n    }\n  }\n  await Promise.all(Array.from(\n    { length: Math.min(limit, items.length) }, worker\n  ));\n  return results;\n}\n// O(n) scheduling work; O(n + min(n, limit)) storage, excluding fn.','Each worker claims a unique index synchronously before awaiting.~At most limit workers run, and results are written by input index.~A caught failure stops future claims and rejects the aggregate. Already-started work is not automatically canceled.','Use a fixed number of worker loops.','Claim an index before awaiting its result.','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Promise/all','// items is a dense array; fn may return a value or Promise.\n// Implement mapLimit(items, limit, fn).')
q('U','Event loop','Microtask error reporting','Follow-up to ASYNC-012: how does an uncaught throw inside queueMicrotask differ from a throw inside Promise.then?','queueMicrotask reports a thrown error as an ordinary uncaught exception. A throw in then rejects the promise returned by then. Attach appropriate rejection handling when using promise reactions.','Both callbacks use microtask scheduling, but their error containers differ. Similar ordering does not imply identical error semantics.','Scheduling and error propagation are separate contracts.','Is there a returned promise to reject?','mdn:Web/API/HTML_DOM_API/Microtask_guide#enqueueing_microtasks')
